course: dictionary
lesson: 2116
title: A word from history and church life
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A convent is a building where nuns live, pray and work together. You may meet this word in history books, in stories, and in the names of schools. Context helps: «his lady-love had gone into a convent» suggests she went there to live with the nuns.
explain: Nuns are women who give their lives to God and take vows. Monks live in a monastery; nuns live in a convent. To check a meaning, ask what word would fit: if the sentence needs a place where nuns live, then convent fits.
explain: The same word can also mean a community of nuns, or a school run by nuns, often called a convent school. Read the words around it to see which meaning the writer chose.
fact: «Convent» is a noun: a convent, convents.
fact: «Convent» means a building where nuns live, pray and work together.
fact: «Convent» can also mean a community of nuns, or a school run by nuns.
fact: Nuns give their lives to God and take vows; Christians believe this is a calling.
fact: Monks live in a monastery; nuns live in a convent.
exercise: «His lady-love had gone into a convent.» Where did she go? | A school for sailors | A pirate ship on the sea | *A place where nuns live, pray and work together | A castle on a hill
exercise: Many schools in Ireland were convent schools. What is a convent school? | A school that only teaches music | A school for sailors | *A school run by nuns | A school with no teachers
exercise: Monks live in a monastery. Where do nuns live? | A field mouse's house | A galley | *A convent | A monastery
test: What do nuns take when they give their lives to God? | *Vows | Field mice | Galleys | Pirate treasure
test: «His lady-love had gone into a convent.» What does convent most likely mean here? | A pirate's den | *A place where nuns live and pray | A ship with oars | A stormy sea
exam: In a convent school, who runs the school? | *Nuns | Corsairs | Field mice | Pirates
